            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "An imitation of silu made from straight-line pieces: the input times hardsigmoid of the input. 0 below -3, the input itself above 3, and a curve (a parabola) in between.\n\nLike hardsigmoid it is cheap to compute and reaches its limits exactly. It was designed for networks running on phones. In a patch it is a relu whose corner is replaced by a short bend.\n\nTHE CURVE:\n0 gives 0. Below -3 it is exactly 0; above 3 exactly the input. Between, it dips to -0.375 at -1.5 and climbs back up - close to silu's shape, with a visible join at -3 and at 3 where the curve meets the straight stretches.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.nn.hardswish\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor to bend. Receiving it triggers the node. A list, number or numpy array is turned into a float32 tensor on the cpu first; a tensor arriving as a tensor keeps its own dtype and device. The curve is applied to every element on its own, so the output has the same shape as the input. An INTEGER tensor (t.arange with whole numbers makes one) is refused: the console reports \"not implemented for 'Long'\" and nothing is sent. Put a t.to float32 in front of it.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\noutput:\nThe bent tensor, the same shape as the input.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nt.linspace makes 128 values running evenly from -5 (left edge of the plot) to 5 (right edge); the middle of the plot is 0. The plot draws two lines over each other: the straight diagonal is the input itself, and the other is the node's output for the same values. Where the two lines coincide the node passes the value unchanged; where they part, that is the bend. The plot's height runs from -1 to 5; the input line runs off the top and bottom where it leaves that range.\n\nRELATED:\nt.nn.silu is the smooth original. t.nn.hardsigmoid is the gate it multiplies by. t.nn.relu6 is what that gate is made from.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 780,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 705,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "An imitation of silu made from straight-line pieces: the input times hardsigmoid of the input. 0 below -3, the input itself above 3, and a curve (a parabola) in between.\n\nLike hardsigmoid it is cheap to compute and reaches its limits exactly. It was designed for networks running on phones. In a patch it is a relu whose corner is replaced by a short bend.\n\nTHE CURVE:\n0 gives 0. Below -3 it is exactly 0; above 3 exactly the input. Between, it dips to -0.375 at -1.5 and climbs back up - close to silu's shape, with a visible join at -3 and at 3 where the curve meets the straight stretches.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.nn.hardswish\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor to bend. Receiving it triggers the node. A list, number or numpy array is turned into a float32 tensor on the cpu first; a tensor arriving as a tensor keeps its own dtype and device. The curve is applied to every element on its own, so the output has the same shape as the input. An INTEGER tensor (t.arange with whole numbers makes one) is refused: the console reports \"not implemented for 'Long'\" and nothing is sent. Put a t.to float32 in front of it.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\noutput:\nThe bent tensor, the same shape as the input.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nt.linspace makes 128 values running evenly from -5 (left edge of the plot) to 5 (right edge); the middle of the plot is 0. The plot draws two lines over each other: the straight diagonal is the input itself, and the other is the node's output for the same values. Where the two lines coincide the node passes the value unchanged; where they part, that is the bend. The plot's height runs from -1 to 5; the input line runs off the top and bottom where it leaves that range.\n\nRELATED:\nt.nn.silu is the smooth original. t.nn.hardsigmoid is the gate it multiplies by. t.nn.relu6 is what that gate is made from."
        },
